{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The target selects city size as the single dominant cross-city modulator, but its own counterconditions expose the flaw: nocturnal UHI intensity is empirically governed by urban canyon geometry (sky view factor, building height-to-width ratio) and impervious surface fraction, not by spatial extent. Dense compact cities with deep street canyons consistently produce larger nocturnal temperature gaps than sprawling low-density cities of equal or greater area, because reduced sky view factor traps longwave radiation. The target's mechanism conflates total thermal mass with per-area energy balance, and its scaling claim fails when per-area storage is held constant.","premise":"Nocturnal UHI intensity is primarily controlled by urban geometry (sky view factor, building height density) and surface radiative properties, not by city spatial extent. Dense compact cities with deep canyons and low sky view factor trap longwave radiation more effectively than sprawling low-rise cities, producing larger nighttime temperature gaps regardless of total area.","counterexample":[{"city":"Hong Kong","area_km2":1106,"nocturnal_UHI_K":7,"sky_view_factor":"very low"},{"city":"Los Angeles","area_km2":1302,"nocturnal_UHI_K":3,"sky_view_factor":"moderate-high"}],"premise_evidence":[{"block":"8a3f7c2d1e9b4f60","quote":"The single dominant cross-city modulator is city size itself: larger cities accumulate more stored thermal mass in their impervious surfaces and generate more anthropogenic heat, creating a mesoscale heat dome whose nocturnal temperature gap scales with the spatial extent of the urbanized area."},{"block":"f2c1b9d4e7a08365","quote":"If the nocturnal UHI correlates more strongly with building height density than with spatial extent, city size is a proxy rather than the causal variable"}],"target_alias":"SRC_001"}]}